# allowed_signers — SSH commit-signing trust list
#
# Git needs to know which public keys may sign before it can report a verified
# signature. That trust list is this file, pointed at by
# `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` in the tracked `.gitconfig`. Without it, `git log
# --format='%G?'` reports `N` for perfectly good SSH-signed commits.
#
# SETUP
#   1. Copy this file to ~/.ssh/allowed_signers
#        cp ~/.dotfiles/examples/allowed-signers-example ~/.ssh/allowed_signers
#   2. Put your principal and your PUBLIC key on one line, then delete this header:
#        <your-principal> ssh-ed25519 <your-public-key>
#   3. Verify it took effect:
#        git log -1 --format='%G? %GS'     # want: G <the principal in this file>
#
# The key is your PUBLIC key - the one in ~/.ssh/*.pub. It is safe to publish: a
# public key can only verify a signature, never create one. Your private key stays
# in ~/.ssh and must never appear here or anywhere tracked.
#
# Get your public key with:  ssh-keygen -y -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
# or copy the contents of   ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
#
# FORMAT (one key per line; the principal precedes the key):
#   <principal> [namespaces="..."] ssh-ed25519 <base64-public-key> [comment]
#
#   <principal> is a LABEL, not a check. Git decides trust by matching the
#   signature to the KEY on the line; it does not compare the principal against
#   the commit's author or committer email. A commit signed by a listed key
#   reports G under any identity, and %GS prints this principal back verbatim -
#   so it confirms what this file says, never that the commit identity matches
#   it. Write the email you author commits with anyway, so the label stays honest
#   and reviewable.
#
# MORE THAN ONE LINE IS FINE — add a line per key you sign with, and keep an
# old key when you rotate so history already signed with it still verifies:
#   you@example.com ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA... old-laptop
#   you@example.com ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA... new-laptop
#
# `namespaces="git"` is what git itself writes and what git reads. Keep it.
#
# This file must NOT contain a private key. If you ever paste a PRIVATE key here,
# remove it from git history: the file is tracked.

# <your-principal> ssh-ed25519 <your-public-key>
